  "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,¬(0\\text{ is not positive}\\text{ and }0\\text{ is not negative}) & ≡¬((0\\text{ is not positive})∧(0\\text{ is not negative})) \\\\  & ≡¬(0\\text{ is not positive})∨¬(0\\text{ is not negative}) \\\\  & ≡(0\\text{ is positive})∨(0\\text{ is negative}) \\\\  & ≡0\\text{ is positive}\\text{ or }0\\text{ is negative}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
